.TH auto-root-check 8

\" auto-man2man (used in generating subcommand lists) depends on this section
.SH NAME
.PP
auto-root-check - Check whether running as root user

\" Convention:
\" Underline anything that is typed verbatim - commands, etc.
.SH SYNOPSIS
.PP
.nf 
.na
auto-root-check calling-script-name ['reason']
.ad
.fi

.SH ARGUMENTS
.nf
.na
calling-script  The calling script name, often just "$0"
reason          Option reason to explain why root privileges are needed
.ad
.fi

.SH DESCRIPTION

The
.B auto-root-check
command checks whether it is being run by the root user.  This
is normally run from a script that requires root privileges, so
that the script can emit a meaningful error message and gracefully
exit rather than continue and allow commands to fail.

The first argument is usually $0, the shell variable indicating
the pathname by which it was invoked.

If the optional 'reason' is provided, it should be enclosed in
quotes, so that it is one argument.

If the using running auto-root-check is not root, it prints
an error message stating that root privileges are necessary,
followed by "reason" if provided.

.SH RETURN VALUES

0 on success, non-zero error codes otherwise.

.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
.na
auto-root-check $0 'Modifies system files'
if ! auto-root-check $0; then
fi
.ad
.fi

